    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Middle Persian, the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> proper, became the prestige and literary language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, used even by non-Persian Iranians, and stayed a prestige language for some time after the empire fell.</li><li>It had shed most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>’s case endings, using prepositions and compound tenses instead, and in its past tenses it treated the doer of an action like a by-phrase, an ergative pattern that New Persian gave up.</li><li>Almost all its literature is Zoroastrian, written mostly in the 9th to 11th centuries in Book Pahlavi, when the language had long ceased to be spoken; so the script’s name, Pahlavi, came to mean the language itself.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Pahlavi scribes wrote many Persian words as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> ones, about a thousand in Book Pahlavi: “me” was written LY, Aramaic for “to me”, but read aloud as the Persian man, and “foot” was written as Aramaic for “his foot” and read pāy.</p></aside>
    </section>

